-- An import cycle, reached indirectly, and accepted - Analysis-Modules.md §2.2.
--
-- Ring imports Loop and Loop imports Ring, so the cycle is closed one module below this one. It
-- used to be rejected, and the reason was real while it lasted: a module was interned before its
-- declarations were resolved, so a module reached a second time while it was still being resolved
-- held only what came before the import that led back to it - and what the program meant depended
-- on which file was named first.
--
-- What removes the reason is that an import no longer triggers the resolution of what it names.
-- The passes run over the whole program, each one finishing for every module before the next
-- starts, so after the declare pass every declaration in the program exists and no pass has to ask
-- whether another module is finished.
--
-- Both directions are exercised, because they are answered by different passes: `ringSize` calls
-- across the cycle, which the signature pass resolves, and `ringOf`/`loopOf` name types across it,
-- which the declare and define passes do.

import Ring
import Loop

fn main() -> Int = ringSize() * 10 + loopOf(Ring {span: 3}).width
